Divya, the daughter of Girish Bisht, was playing with other children under a mango tree near her house when the leopard appeared and attacked her. Her mother Kavita and her grandparents were away working on their farm.
The leopard dragged Divya to a nearby bush. Children, who were playing with her, raised an alarm and informed local villagers about the incident.
Soon, Kavita and other villagers rushed to the spot. Forest and revenue officials also reached the spot and started a search operation. Later, her body was recovered from a nearby bush, said Arya.
Divya was a Grade II student and her father works in a private firm in Delhi, he added.
Arya was informed by local villagers about a leopard that had been on the prowl in the area for the past three months and had killed a few cattle and dogs.
Bisht, a member of Bhikiasain nagar panchayat, demanded an immediate setting up of a cage to catch the leopard.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1620 chars)"
"16 sheep killed, 17 injured in leopard attack in J&K’s Budgam - Bilkul Online",2026-09-10,Jammu & Kashmir,Budgam,Leopard,Livestock Attack,16.0,17.0,0.0,Leopard attacked and killed livestock in Budgam.,0.98,single_incident,,Bilkul Online,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiowFBVV95cUxQS0tKRW1iSnpqVTZDQUVfT0ZNNTVCRXBBNXdfNlk3Z1lYdTlYQi1sUkN1Y0plLV9RQjhhNGpSMmtrV0t1V0Q2X2toT0p4NkVrbXlZSHFXWXM2NmM4akFlVnNrdlNNeGNNMlFSc0NVOUFiRENjWTI5TndKdjdidVNxTFd0ay1TR2RhazVlSElmRUFyUWF3QTJaUlQ2bG5MMWd6dmZr?oc=5,,,"Srinagar
As many as 16 sheep were killed and 17 others injured in a purported leopard attack on a flock in J&K’s Budgam district early on Thursday. The flock was attacked in the Chadoora area of Budgam during the intervening night of Wednesday and Thursday. The owner of the flock, Imtiyaz Ahmad Chopan, told some reporters that a leopard had attacked his sheep, though the animal’s identity is yet to be confirmed. He said the animal entered the livestock enclosure in Badipora village of Chadoora during the night and attacked the flock. “We depend on livestock for our livelihood, and the incident has caused significant financial losses for us,” he said.
The flock owner appealed to the government and departments concerned to provide immediate assistance and compensation for the loss of livestock. Meanwhile, locals also urged the Wildlife Department to inspect the site and take necessary measures to prevent further attacks in the area. Wild predators like Asiatic black bears and leopards frequently enter human settlements in Jammu and Kashmir due to severe habitat loss, climate change, and dwindling food sources in the wild. Between 2020 and mid-2025, Jammu and Kashmir recorded over 17,200 human-wildlife conflict incidents, resulting in more than 100 deaths and nearly 1,100 injuries. Asiatic black bears and leopards cause the majority of attacks, straying into villages, agricultural fields, and apple orchards.
The chief reasons for human-animal conflict in J&K are unplanned development, deforestation, and the conversion of buffer zones into residential areas or orchards, which shrink natural spaces. Open garbage dumps, livestock, and abundant fruit in orchards near forest fringes attract predators repeatedly. Irregular weather and shifting snowfall patterns reduce natural forage and water availability in higher elevations, forcing animals down into human habitations.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1894 chars)"
"Uttarakhand grapples with a black bear problem: 5 people killed in attacks, authorities scramble for solutions - The Indian Express",2025-11-22,Uttarakhand,,Asiatic Black Bear,Human Death,0.0,0.0,5.0,A series of attacks by black bears resulted in multiple human fatalities.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,,The Indian Express,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Uttarakhand grapples with a black bear problem: 5 people killed in attacks, authorities scramble for solutions
Climate change, habitat degradation among reasons cited by forest department for increased conflict between humans and the Asiatic black bear
Following a spate of attacks by Asiatic black bears in Uttarakhand, the forest department has released a slew of directions, including shooting to kill as a last resort. Five people have died in these attacks this year.
The guidelines for the prevention and management of human–bear conflict during the winter season were issued on Thursday. This is the pre-hibernation period for bears, when bears that hibernate during winter are found active in several areas, the department said. It attributed the possible causes for changes in bear behaviour to climate change, reduced food availability, improper waste management, and disturbance to habitat.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 901 chars)"
Three Bear Attacks In Srinagar In 24 Hours Underscore Rising Human-Wildlife Conflict Cases In J&K - ETV Bharat,2026-08-17,Jammu & Kashmir,Srinagar,Asiatic Black Bear,Human Injury,0.0,0.0,0.0,"Multiple bear attacks reported, highlighting increasing conflicts.",0.98,aggregate_period_total,,ETV Bharat,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Three Bear Attacks In Srinagar In 24 Hours Underscore Rising Human-Wildlife Conflict Cases In J&K
Locals in Khonmoh area of Srinagar are in panic due to three back to back attacks that has left three residents injured.
Srinagar: In an alarming incident marking the rising human-wildlife conflict cases, as many as three persons were mauled by a bear on the outskirts of Srinagar city of Jammu and Kashmir causing panic among the residents.
According to officials, a black bear attacked a woman Fatima, 57, wife of Muhammad Maqbool Wani near her house at Shah Mohalla of Khonmoh area of the city on Monday morning leaving her injured. After immediate first aid, Fatima was shifted to a nearby hospital for treatment.
Another incident on Monday morning took place in Sirhindi Colony of the same area where the same beast injured 30-year-old Muzaffar Ahmed Butt, son of Ghulam Hassan Butt. Butt is also undergoing treatment at the hospital.
Both these incidents took place a day after the bear attacked a local resident Ijaz Ahmed Butt, son of Abdul Rashid Butt person in the Shiv Dikri area of Khonmoh. He was injured in the attack, after which his family immediately shifted him to the hospital.
After the continuous attacks, the authorities have deployed teams of the Wildlife Department in the area. Officials said that they have taken notice of the incidents and are continuously monitoring the situation. An official of the Wildlife Department said that the bear has been located, adding it will be rescued soon.
Three attacks in less than 24 hours have increased the concerns of the people living in the area, where many houses are located near forest areas.
According to data compiled by the Jammu and Kashmir Wildlife Protection Department, more than 17,200 incidents of human-wildlife collisions were recorded from 2020 to July 2025. More than 100 people were killed and nearly 1,100 injured in these incidents.
The latest attacks in Khonmoh come about a month after a man was killed by a black bear while working in an orchard in Kulgam district of south Kashmir. Earlier in June, a casual labourer was also injured in a bear attack while on night duty at the Royal Springs Golf Course in Srinagar.
