"Human-wildlife conflict: 2 killed, over 40 injured in bear attacks across Kashmir since apple harvest began - Greater Kashmir",2026-09-07,Jammu & Kashmir,,Asiatic Black Bear,Human Death,0.0,40.0,2.0,Bear attacks resulted in 2 deaths and over 40 injuries in Kashmir.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,,Greater Kashmir,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"More than 40 people have been injured and two killed in bear attacks across Kashmir since apple harvesting began in August, with animals increasingly straying into orchards, maize fields and nearby settlements.
South Kashmir has recorded 12 injuries and one death in the Anantnag-Kulgam wildlife range, while the Pulwama-Shopian range has reported eight injuries so far, taking the total in the region to 20.
Central Kashmir has recorded eight injuries, and north Kashmir has recorded 16 injuries and one death.
In Kulgam, 45-year-old Latifa Banoo was mauled to death on Aug. 31 while working in an orchard at Tarigam village.
Her family learned of the attack only after she was shifted to SDH Qaimoh by the villagers.
“We are devastated by her death. Authorities should step in to prevent such incidents and ensure that no more lives are lost,” her family said.
Days earlier, 68-year-old Abdul Salam Teeli of Ahmadabad village in the D.H. Pora area of the same district was seriously injured after going to the aid of another orchard worker, Bilal Ahmad Kohli, who had cried for help.
“When I heard the cries, I rushed to help him, along with my wife and daughter-in-law. I saw Kohli lying injured. The bear then turned towards me and attacked me as well,” Teeli said.
“Such a time should never come again in my life. The moment was like a heart attack.”
This spike in attacks has been reported across Kashmir
In the latest attacks, Mohammad Maqbool Bhat was seriously injured at Mirmadan in Dooru, Anantnag, on Sep 3, while another man was injured at Lalwani Seer village of the same district today, on Sep 6.
He was immediately shifted to GMC Anantnag, from where he has been referred to SKIMS, Srinagar.
Wildlife Warden Anantnag-Kulgam Sajad Bhat said August and September are particularly sensitive because ripening apples and maize draw bears into areas where people are working.
In Pulwama, Ghulam Nabi Bhat, 55, a flour-mill worker from Jawbehara, Awantipora was also grievously injured, at his workplace.
Six more people were also injured in separate bear attacks in the Tral area.
In Shopian, a minor girl was injured in a leopard attack in Dragad village on August 4.
Earlier, towards the end of July, three people, including a woman were injured in a bear attack in Reshipora-Harmain in the same district.
Central Kashmir saw three people mauled at Khonmoh, Srinagar, within 24 hours on Aug 16 and 17.
In Budgam’s Rawatpora-Khag, a man, Abdul Qayoom Thokar was critically injured in a bear attack, while in Ganderbal Mohammad Maqbool Bhat was attacked by the animal at Watalbagh , leaving him grievously injured.
In north Kashmir’s Kupwara district, 6-year-old Subahat Jan was killed in a bear attack at Nichama village.
In Bandipora, Javid Ahmad Pir was mauled at Kralgund-Hajin, while Farooq Ahmad Khan suffered severe facial injuries at Chrawan in Ajas.
In Baramula, a man Firdous Ahmad Mir and a woman Zahida Begum were injured while cutting grass at Janwara in Sopore.
“Last year, also during the apple harvest season, we lived in constant fear of wildlife attacks, and this year is no different,” said orchardist Mohammad Sultan of Kupwara.
Wildlife Warden North Kashmir Suhail Intesar said August and September are the peak months for bear encounters.
“Once apples ripen, bears come down to orchards. This has become an annual phenomenon,” Intesar said.
Leopard sightings have also increased, with animals turning up closer to villages and homes. A child was injured in a leopard attack in Shopian.
Wildlife Warden Pulwama-Shopian Suhail Wagay warned against leaving children alone in areas where leopards have been sighted.
“Children are mostly their prey, so they should not be left alone, particularly in areas where leopards have been sighted,” Wagay said.
Wildlife teams have tranquilised and captured many animals, while others were driven away before they could cause harm. In Pulwama and Shopian alone, 46 animals have been removed from human habitations since the harvest began - 22 black bears, 21 leopards and three others.
“These animals were actually present in human habitations. We responded immediately, rescued them and did not allow them to cause any damage due to the quick response,” Wagay said.
Control rooms have been activated across wildlife divisions. Orchard workers and residents have been advised to move in groups, avoid fields during evening and after-dark hours, carry torches and report sightings immediately.
With the harvest continuing through November, officials have urged people living and working near forest fringes to remain vigilant.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 4593 chars)"
"Leopard kills one, injures five in multiple attacks across Himachal’s Mandi district - newindianexpress.com",2025-12-24,Himachal Pradesh,Mandi,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,5.0,1.0,Leopard attacks resulted in one fatality and five injuries in Mandi.,0.98,single_incident,,newindianexpress.com,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"CHANDIGARH: One person was killed and five others injured after a leopard carried out a series of attacks at three different locations in the Balh Ghati area of Himachal Pradesh’s Mandi district on Tuesday morning before being shot dead by locals, officials said.
The leopard attacked people in Chandiyal, Bhadyal and Malwana villages, triggering panic among residents. According to officials, the animal first struck at Chandiyal, after which it moved to Bhadyal, injuring several people, and finally reached Malwana village, where it mauled a 40-year-old man, Balveer Singh, resulting in his death.
Singh, a resident of Bholi village, was a migrant worker living in Mandi and had come to Malwana to visit relatives. All those attacked sustained paw-related injuries and were immediately shifted to hospital, where doctors described their condition as stable.
Sources said forest department teams were rushed to the affected villages soon after the first alerts were received. As panic and chaos gripped the area, villagers reportedly came out of their homes armed with sticks and sharp implements in an attempt to protect themselves.
The leopard was eventually killed when it tried to attack again. Its carcass has been taken into custody and sent for post-mortem examination.
The injured have been identified as Sahib Singh, a native of Bihar; Dinanath of Bhadyal; Rekha Devi, wife of Dinanath; Janit, also from Bhadyal; and Champa Devi of Malwana. They have been admitted to Shri Lal Bahadur Shastri Government Medical College and Hospital, Nerchowk, where they are undergoing treatment.
Confirming the leopard attack, an official of the local administration said one person had died, five others were injured and the animal had also been killed.
Following the incident, forest officials have intensified surveillance in and around the affected villages. Locals have been advised to avoid venturing out alone during early morning and late evening hours, when wildlife movement is more likely.
An inquiry is underway to determine how and why the leopard entered densely populated areas, once again highlighting the growing human–wildlife conflict in the region.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2164 chars)"
"Leopard attack in Himachal’s Mandi leaves one dead, nine injured; animal killed by villagers - Telegraph India",2025-12-24,Himachal Pradesh,Mandi,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,9.0,1.0,A leopard attack resulted in one death and nine injuries in Mandi.,0.98,single_incident,,Telegraph India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,,extraction_failed(page_fetch_failed after resolved_via_decode)
Leopard declared as man-eater after two women killed in Nainital | Hindustan Times - Hindustan Times,2023-12-12,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,2.0,Leopard declared man-eater after killing two women.,0.98,single_incident,,Hindustan Times,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Leopard declared as man-eater after two women killed in Nainital
The Uttarakhand forest department has deployed four cages, 10 camera traps and 75 forest personnel in the operation to eliminate the man-eater leopard
The Uttarakhand forest department has declared a leopard as a man-eater after the animal killed two women in Nainital district last week, officials said on Tuesday. The department has also roped in hunters to eliminate the leopard, they added..
According to forest officials, on December 7, the leopard killed Indra Devi, a resident of Maluwatal in Nainital district. Two days later, on December 9, Pushpa Devi, a 38-year-old resident of Pinrau village, who was cutting grass in the field near her house, was also killed in a leopard attack while another woman, 28-year-old Urmila Devi was injured in the attack.
Angered by this, the villagers demanded that the leopard be declared a man-eater and eliminated. Angry villagers also gheraoed Nainital DFO Chandrashekhar Joshi’s office. On Monday, the forest department declared the leopard maneater and issued orders to kill it.
Chandrashekhar Joshi, divisional forest officer (DFO) of Nainital forest division said, “The leopard which was creating terror in Maluvatal and Pinarau villages has been declared maneater. Forest teams under the leadership of veterinarian Dr. Himanshu Pangti are patrolling the area. Hunters Harish Dhami and Vipin Chandra have arrived to eliminate the leopard.”
“Four cages, 10 camera traps, 75 forest personnel have been deployed in this operation to eliminate the problematic leopard,” he said.
He said, “Apart from this, forest teams have been deployed in Hairakhan and Dhungshil areas also. SDRF (State Disaster Response Force) team has also been deployed in the area.”
He further said, “Patrolling is being done in half a dozen villages including Pinero, Maluvatal. Villagers are also forming groups and helping us in patrolling.”
The movement of single devotees has been banned to Chhota Kailash temple. Citing security, the forest department and administration have appealed to the devotees not to come to the temple for a few days, said officials.
Pramod Kumar, sub-district magistrate of Nainital said, “There is a ban on a single person going to the temple. There is no restriction if devotees go in groups.”
Over 450 people have been killed in leopard attacks in the state since the state was formed in 2000. Human deaths in leopard attacks account for nearly half of the total deaths due to wild animals in the state, according to the data of the state forest department. According to wildlife experts, there are many factors due to which it has been difficult to check man-leopard conflict in the state, be it the hilly terrain which provides ample space for leopards to hide to human settlements scattered in hills overlapping with the leopard territories.
In May last year, angry villagers burnt alive a leopard in Pauri district after the leopard killed a woman. In March 2011, angry villagers had burnt alive a leopard in the same district. The leopard had attacked ten people in the area. In June 2018, angry villagers also set a forest on fire in Bageshwar district after a 7-year-old boy was killed by a leopard.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 3226 chars)"
Leopard kills 13-year-old girl in Uttarakhand's New Tehri - Deccan Herald,2024-10-19,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Leopard killed a 13-year-old girl in Uttarakhand.,0.98,single_incident,,Deccan Herald,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisAFBVV95cUxOcS1VcUdnUXJFWE9DbmFDWUpFOFFZeU5CT0xrN21hekZVLVBjSHJuSk1KSlVNSUNGSFBkck9BYzNidGVJNkhtcmJCX3BHR05tSUd5eHFwcXY0cGNlb2lNdG5LU2FseENQTEVxR1dnY2hxbHJIcllXSi0xQ2VWT0xWV3dpRGZVanR6aVk5akRYbDlfLXc2cTFoZlBZb3owa29GNDVEek9tR0JQMEl0dUJodw?oc=5,,,"Leopard kills 13-year-old girl in Uttarakhand's New Tehri
In view of the incidents, the forest department has deployed two shooters in the area to kill the leopard, and eight cameras have been installed to track its activities. Cages have also been set up in the hopes of catching the leopard.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 293 chars)"
Woman mauled to death in tiger attack in Champawat - The Times of India,2025-03-29,Uttarakhand,,Tiger,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,A woman was mauled to death in a tiger attack.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Rudrapur: A 45-year-old woman was mauled to death by a tiger near Devipura-Majhgaon village in Champawat district on Wednesday. The attack occurred in the Khatima range forest, about 2km from the village, while she was collecting firewood with five other women. 
The tiger attacked Virma Devi from behind and dragged her deep into the forest. A search operation was launched by a forest department team after the women accompanying Devi rushed back to inform villagers. Devi's mutilated body was found in the bushes three hours later, about 500m from where she was attacked. After a post-mortem exam at Tanakpur hospital in Champawat, her body was handed over to her family, said Surendra Singh Koranga, SHO of Banbasa police station. 
Khatima sub-divisional forest officer Sachita Verma said evidence from the site confirmed a tiger attack. She added that necessary measures were being taken to ensure public safety. 
Residents in the area said they were afraid to step out of their homes, especially in the evenings, as this is the second tiger-related death in the region in two months. In Feb, a 35-year-old man was killed while grazing cattle near Tukdi village in Nanakmatta area of Udham Singh Nagar district. ""We demand immediate action to capture the tiger before another tragedy occurs,"" said Deepak Chandra Rajwar, head administrator of Devipura. The forest department has intensified patrolling in the area and plans to install a cage to trap the tiger. 
Since its formation in 2000, Uttarakhand has recorded a high number of fatalities due to tiger attacks. From Jan 2000 to Dec 2023, 551 people were killed and over 1,833 injured in leopard and tiger attacks combined. 
The number of tiger-related deaths has fluctuated in recent years. In 2022, 11 people were killed, which doubled to 22 in 2023. Seven such deaths were reported in 2024. With Uttarakhand's tiger population increasing from 442 in 2018 to 560, human-wildlife conflict has risen, driving the need for urgent and effective strategies to ensure the safety of both humans and wildlife.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2062 chars)"
"Schools closed, night curfew in Uttarakhand villages after tiger kills 2 - The Times of India",2023-04-18,Uttarakhand,,Tiger,Human Death,0.0,0.0,2.0,"Two people were killed by a tiger, leading to school closures.",0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"DEHRADUN: After two elderly men, a farmer and a retired schoolteacher, both in their 70s, were mauled to death in almost back-to-back tiger attacks within 72 hours on the fringes of the Kalagarh Tiger Reserve (KTR), a worried local administration on Monday decided to impose night curfew from 7pm to 6am, and close schools in the area for two days.
With the tiger having been often spotted lurking near villages in Rikhnikhal and Nainidanda blocks of Pauri district, just north of the tiger reserve, people have been warned against venturing out after dark.
Speaking about the terror caused by the tiger, Laxmi Devi, a village pradhan told TOI, “Even the local MLA, Dilip Rawat, spotted the tiger while visiting the area. Staying indoors cannot be a long-term solution. How will our children go to school? All of us are in extreme fear.”
In the first attack on April 13, a 73-year-old farmer, Birendra Singh, along with his wife was harvesting his wheat crop at Dalla village in Rikhnikhal block of Pauri district and had taken a break when a tiger pounced upon him. Despite his wife’s desperate cries for help, the animal dragged away the farmer. Later, villagers found the man's mutilated body some distance away.
In the second incident, which happened barely 35km away from Dalla, the half-eaten body of a 75-year-old retired schoolteacher, 
Ranveer Singh
, was found near his house in Simli village in Nainidanda block on April 15.
Madhya Pradesh: Tigress and her 4 cubs spotted crossing road in Panna, video goes viral
The Kalagarh Tiger Reserve falls within the Jim Corbett National Park, which has among the highest population density of tigers in the country.
Villagers in the area are pressing for the animal to be declared a man-eater and immediately put down. Responding to the demands, DFO of Pauri Garhwal, Swapnil Anirudha, said, “We have got the go-ahead to trap and tranquilise the animal. Our teams have installed cages and over a dozen camera traps have been set up. Patrolling teams are actively combing the place. Also, families of both the victims have been provided a preliminary compensation of about Rs 1.2 lakh each.”
Explained: How India revived its tiger population
There is also speculation that the killings may be the work of more than one tiger, with villagers claiming to have spotted multiple sub-adult tigers in the area.
Forest officials said that DNA samples of both the victims were sent to the Centre for Cellular and Molecular Platforms in Bengaluru to check if the killings were carried out by the same tiger.
A senior forest official, who has been monitoring the landscape of the area for several years, added that “the incidents point at the fact that these big cats are being driven out of Corbett by more dominant tigers and they must be in search of new territory as they are sub-adults. Hence, they have strayed into these villages.""
Incidentally, angry villagers in Rikhnikhal had burnt alive a suspected man-eater leopard in 2011, triggering concerns among forest officials of a repeat of that act this time around.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 3063 chars)"
Leopard kills bovine inside a farmhouse visited by tourists - The Times of India,2023-10-21,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Livestock Attack,1.0,0.0,0.0,A leopard killed livestock inside a farmhouse.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"NAVI MUMBAI: A leopard killed a cow inside a farmhouse in Saygaon village in the tourist hotspot of Shrivardhan taluka, Raigad on Wednesday. The incident spread fear among locals and tourists. The attack was caught on CCTV. Few other animals were standing around when the leopard attacked the sleeping cow. This is the second such incident in the last four months.
A two-year-old dead cow is among the other cows who were tied in the open area inside the farmhouse run by Amit and Mansi Kamath.
The CCTV footage shows that while the leopard was attacking the cow, two other cows were tied nearby. One of the cows tried to escape but to no avail. After the attack the leopard left the farmhouse. The cow was not providing milk.
The farmhouse workers had alerted the owners, who in turn told to the forest department. The forest team conducted a panchnama after due inspection. They also scanned the CCTV footage. Accordingly, warnings and awareness about the leopard movement has been issued to villages.
The range forest officer (territorial)-Shrivardhan Sanjay Pandharkame, said, “The cows were not tied and kept in herds inside the cattle shed. Wild animals easily attack cattle when they are moving open or those who break away from their herd. We have observed that people are tying domestic animals in the open in the forest area. There were no tourists in the farmhouse that day.” Assistant conservator of forest (territorial) Vishwajeet Jadhav said, “A three-member team comprising a forest guard, agricultural officer, and Agricultural Produce and Livestock Market Committee (APMC) decides the market and compensation values of the killed domestic animal. Accordingly, 75% of the compensation value will be released to the owner soon.”
We also published the following articles recently
Supreme court sends notice to all states on cow vigilantismThe Supreme Court has asked all states to respond to claims by the women's wing of CPI that hate crimes and alleged lynching of Muslims by cow vigilante groups are increasing in India. The court had previously taken note of the issue three months ago. The National Federation of Indian Women informed the court that people transporting meat and cattle are being attacked by vigilante groups, and then the injured persons are being accused by the police. The court had previously sought responses from the Centre and six states on the issue. Rajasthan elections: Cow remains a poll issue, and a bother for farmersAs the assembly poll approaches in the Mewat region, the cow remains a major issue. BJP is focusing on the cow and Hindutva agenda to win the election, while Congress hopes to gain support from Muslims and Dalits. Alwar district, known for cow smuggling and mob lynching, has seen incidents of cattle smuggling and farmers facing difficulties due to unclaimed cattle damaging their crops. Farmers resort to ""cow surgical strikes"" to protect their fields, but face the risk of being accused as smugglers. Uttarakhand: Tiger kills forest guard in CorbettA forest guard was fatally attacked by a tiger while on patrol duty in the Kalagrah range of Corbett Tiger Reserve. The incident took place in the Paterpani area, where the guard, Pawan Kumar, was suddenly attacked by the tiger. Despite being rushed to a medical facility, Kumar did not survive the attack.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 3323 chars)"
"Fearing leopard attack, Uttarkashi villagers want schools to not re-open - The Times of India",2026-03-11,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,A man was killed by a leopard in Uttarakhand.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"UTTARKASHI/DEHRADUN: Leopard scare continues to intensify in Uttarakhand, with the situation turning worrisome in some villages in Uttarkashi. 
Residents of four villages - Tulyada, Dharkot, Khalsi and Chamiyari -- in Chinyalisaur block of Uttarkashi have urged authorities in the education department to not resume schools post- summer vacation, fearing leopard attacks.
They have also sought assistance from the district administration as schools are all set to reopen from Saturday (July 1). 
Sensing the fear among the villagers, the authorities have agreed to resume classes in government buildings within the villages, like the panchayat ghar, rather than asking children to walk through the forest area to reach school. 
Nearly 50 schools are impacted by the threat of the big cats. The forest and district administration teams, which are scanning the area, said that ""the presence of five leopards is anticipated in a 5-km stretch."" 
""Badi Mani and Barkot saw two incidents in the past 1.5 months of women being killed by big cats. Patrolling in the 5km stretch there shows that there are five leopards in the area,"" said one of the forest team members involved in the operation. 
GS Negi, block education officer of Chinyalisaur, Uttarkashi, said, ""The safety of school children -- particularly those who are studying in schools located near the forest or in secluded places -- is of prime concern for us. Considering this, we are thinking of running the schools in government buildings located in the village.""
He added that some schools are located on the border of the villages and are comparatively safer. 
""We may ask parents to drop and pick the children from school. We have informed senior officials about the situation and will take necessary action soon,"" said Negi.
Recently, a woman was attacked by a leopard in Ranikhet but managed to save herself using a sickle. Similarly, an elderly woman was attacked by another leopard in Aabki village.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1963 chars)"
"Woman killed in leopard attack in Nainital, third incident in two weeks - Rediff",2026-01-11,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Another woman killed in a leopard attack in Nainital.,0.98,single_incident,,Rediff,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"A woman was mauled to death by a leopard attack in Dhari block of Uttarakhand's Nainital district on Sunday, officials said. 
This is the third incident such incident in the district in a fortnight. 	 The officials said the incident occurred around noon in Dhanachuli area of Khutiyakhal when Ganga Devi (35) had gone into the forest to collect fodder for her cattle. 
Devi's body was recovered two kilometers inside the forest. 
A forest department team reached the spot after being informed of the incident. 
The death of the woman in the leopard attack has caused tension and anger among the local people. 
The locals alleged that despite repeated sightings of a leopard in the area for the past few days, the forest department had not taken any precautionary measures for the safety of the people. 
Earlier, two similar incidents of leopard attacks had occurred on December 26 last year in Dhari block and on December 30 in Okhalakanda block, in which two women were killed. -- PTI","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 985 chars)"
Leopard attacks 12-year-old boy in Dehradun - Deccan Herald,2024-01-14,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A 12-year-old boy was injured in a leopard attack.,0.98,single_incident,,Deccan Herald,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinwFBVV95cUxPVENDYS1MVm8tRkxESzRHY3ZnX0xOOE1JQ3RUN3RBQlJRc0RYX19uTklobVB5NExYZFE3NjM4cDlBb3NJdWtGV1VRaGxFNkk0UEMxY0RoZDJheFV4Q0MyMUZ2d3JpZ3hVTWF0V04tZXFrbFlXR2hqSlpONVQ3TFlydUdZbXVsZ0Y4ODFkcnBrLU9MYkFjdEttaDNzQVlmWVU?oc=5,,,"Leopard attacks 12-year-old boy in Dehradun
After the incident came to light, Dehradun Senior Superintendent of Police Ajay Singh gave instructions to increase patrolling in the area and police are cautioning people living nearby using loudspeakers, they said.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 260 chars)"
Shoot-to-kill order for Bhimtal leopard - The Times of India,2023-12-13,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,0.0,0.0,Authorities issued a shoot-to-kill order for a leopard after multiple incidents.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"NAINITAL: The state forest department on Tuesday issued orders to eliminate the leopard suspected to have killed two women over the last five days. Armed patrol teams have now set out to hunt down the predator. 
The villagers of Panori in Bhimtal block are in fear as many residents have reported encountering the big cat during routine tasks. Locals and the village head said these were the first instances of leopard attacks in the area in many generations on record.
Villagers are also dealing with a dual challenge involving access to water and fodder. The village relies on a water source situated in a dense forest, but for the past two days, they have been requesting the assistance of forest officials to accompany them to fetch water. Despite the villagers being armed, the officials have been unwilling to join them. Additionally, the villagers have sought cattle fodder for their livestock, but their request has gone unanswered. On Tuesday morning, when a group of women attempted to collect fodder from the forest, they encountered a large feline and were forced to retreat. 
Bhimtal divisional forest officer (DFO) Chandrashekhar Joshi confirmed that they have received the directive to eliminate the killer leopard. He said, ""Teams armed with weapons have been deployed to tranquilise or kill the leopard and we are also installing surveillance cameras in the area to monitor the big cat's movements."" 
Chief Conservator of Forests (CCF) Sanjeev Chaturvedi said leopards have been prowling in the hills of Uttarakhand of late. He said, ""The presence of leopards, once thought to be exclusive to plains and dense forests, underscores a positive trend in wildlife conservation efforts. Recent documentation reveals the adaptation of these elusive creatures to hilly terrains, challenging established beliefs.""
This year, the presence of leopards has been documented in at least three instances. In February, a leopard was found dead in Jhadgaon, Almora district. In April, camera traps captured images of a male and female leopard moving around in Dalla village in Rikhnikhal block in Pauri Garhwal district. Recently, leopard sightings have also been reported in Jageshwar, Almora, Bhimtal and Nainital.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2217 chars)"
"Bear attacks on rise in Kashmir as apple orchards, maize fields become hotspots - Kashmir News Observer",,,,,,,,,,,single_incident,unrecognized_conflict_type:; report_type_backfilled_by_heuristic,Kashmir News Observer,https://news.google.?oc=5,2026-08-26,,"Kulgam, Aug 26 (KNO): For 62-year-old Abdul Salam Teeli, a resident of Ahmadabad village of Damhal Hanjipora, Kulgam, what began as an ordinary morning in his apple orchard quickly turned into a fight for survival. Teeli said he left his home around 11 am and reached his orchard around 11:30 am along with his wife and daughter-in-law. “Shortly after reaching the orchard, I heard that a bear had attacked a person nearby. When I heard the cries, I tried to run away with my wife and daughter-in-law. The bear then turned towards me and attacked me as well,” he told the news agency—Kashmir News Observer (KNO). Recalling the frightening moments, Teeli said the animal came close before attacking him, leaving him with injuries to his head. “Such a time should never come again in my life. The moment was like a heart attack,” he said. Teeli was shifted to a hospital for treatment. The other person injured in the attack was also taken to a medical facility. A growing number of bear attacks across Kashmir have triggered concern among residents, particularly those living close to forests, orchards and agricultural areas, with wildlife experts attributing the rising human-bear encounters to habitat disturbance, easy availability of food and increasing human activity around wildlife habitats. From south Kashmir to Srinagar and north Kashmir, bear attacks are on the rise, leaving people injured and, in some cases, creating fear among the population dependent on orchards and fields. Dozens of people have suffered injuries so far this year, while some have suffered fatal casualties. According to official statistics, 32 people have been killed while 350 injured in wild animal attacks in Jammu and Kashmir over two years. A total of 9,301 wild animal attacks were reported across the Union Territory in 2023–24, in which 18 people were killed and 137 injured. The highest number of attacks, 1,444, was reported from Jammu district, followed by 1,173 in the border district of Kupwara in north Kashmir, 998 in Kishtwar, 950 in Baramulla, 826 in Doda, 756 in Ramban, 750 in Bandipora, 667 in Anantnag, 367 in Pulwama, 230 in Udhampur, 224 in Shopian, 218 in Budgam, 135 in Kulgam, 110 in Rajouri, 92 in Ganderbal, 86 in Srinagar, 70 in Poonch and 15 in Reasi. In 2024–25, a total of 6,360 wild animal attacks were reported in the Union Territory, in which 14 people lost their lives, and 213 were injured. The highest number of attacks in 2024–25 occurred in Jammu, with 1,341 incidents, followed by 686 in Ramban, 673 in Kishtwar, 637 in Anantnag, 609 in Doda, 381 in Pulwama, 311 in Udhampur, 309 in Budgam, 252 in Kupwara, 219 in Shopian, 182 in Baramulla, 174 in Kathua, 153 in Bandipora, 143 in Kulgam, 82 each in Ganderbal and Poonch, 80 in Rajouri, and 46 in Srinagar. Over 50 control rooms have been set up across J&K to provide a continuous response to wildlife emergencies and conflict situations. These control rooms are equipped with a range of specialised tools, including tranquillising guns, net guns, snake-catching sticks, protective gear such as helmets and gloves, and cages of various sizes. Some facilities also have night-vision binoculars, camera traps, GPS devices, walkie-talkies, searchlights, surveillance drones and vehicles, and are staffed by trained personnel for immediate action. Most of the attacks took place in the morning hours when people arrived to work in orchards and agricultural lands, an official said, adding that the most vulnerable areas are those with the highest tree coverage and the largest extent of protected areas, including forests, riverine vegetation and meadows that support a more varied prey base. Wildlife researcher and conservationist Kashif Farooq Bhat said the increase in human-bear encounters during this period should be viewed in the context of habitat disturbance and seasonal food availability. “There could be many reasons that human-bear interactions are more, but this is nothing new as we have been seeing it over the past years,” Bhat told KNO. According to him, disturbance in natural habitats and the availability of food outside forests are among the possible reasons. He explained: “We are observing that bears are roaming in cities and villages for days. The question is, what does it feed on? We throw a lot of household garbage and horticultural waste. This is the apple harvesting season in Kashmir, and those apples could be the prime attraction for bears. Maize is also available in the same season, so it is quite evident that during the harvesting season human-bear interactions can be more."" Bhat said land surrounding protected areas that was traditionally used as grazing land has increasingly been converted into agricultural and horticultural fields as well as human settlements. “That means we have entered a little into the national park, and growing agricultural and horticultural crops gives an open invitation to bears to come to these lands,” he said. The researcher said the nature of human-bear interaction has also changed over the years. “Earlier, when bears approached these grazing lands, people would scare them away. But the story is entirely different now,” he said. Anthropogenic disturbance inside and around wildlife habitats, coupled with careless disposal of household and horticultural waste, could be among the major drivers of conflict, he added. On whether the bear population itself has increased, Bhat said the number of attacks cannot be used to establish a population increase. “As far as the population of Asiatic black bears is concerned, we cannot declare whether the population has increased or decreased on the basis of human-bear interactions,” he said, adding that scientific population estimation is required to establish the actual status of the species. “Once those scientific studies are conducted, the reasons for major conflict will be clear,” he said, adding that anthropogenic disturbance cannot be ruled out as a major driver of human-bear interactions. According to Bhat, areas surrounding protected forests are particularly vulnerable, especially where apple orchards, maize and bean cultivation provide easily accessible food. “Food remains highly available, and bears most often visit those areas,” he said. Bhat said local communities have a key role in reducing human-bear encounters. He advised people not to encroach upon, alter or disturb natural wildlife habitats and stressed the need for proper disposal of household and horticultural waste rather than dumping it along roadsides or in open areas. People living close to protected areas should keep outdoor lights on and avoid allowing children and women to move alone outside, particularly after sunset, he advised. Bhat urged residents to follow directions issued by the Wildlife Protection Department. “Most importantly, if a bear is sighted in the locality, people should stay calm, go out in groups and not alone, inform the Department of Wildlife and not take the matter into their own hands,” he added—(KNO)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 7020 chars)"
