Fear grips Mussoorie residents as leopard attacks hotel worker on street - The Times of India,2016-11-18,Uttarakhand,Mussoorie,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,Leopard attacks hotel worker on street in Mussoorie.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"MUSSOORIE: Fear has gripped the residents of Mussoorie after a 34-year-old man was attacked by a leopard on the busy Gandhi Chowk. Monu Thakur, who works at a local hotel, was returning home after work when the big cat pounced on him on Wednesday night. Thakur told TOI that he fought off the animal with his bare hands and is glad that he lived to tell the tale.
“I had seen the leopard near my employer’s residence in Gandhi Chowk earlier in the evening. We had used crackers and sticks to scare it away. But the leopard sprang up on me later that night when I was walking past that route. If it wasn’t for my jacket, the animal’s claws would have dug deep into my skin. I held the front paws of the animal to save myself. At the same time, I took the support of the railing I was leaning on and pushed the leopard. It fell back and I ran for my life.”
 Captain Kamaljit Singh, the owner of the hotel and near whose residence the attack took place, said that he had sighted a leopard cub in the evening. “I’ve seen leopards in forests but to see it on a busy location like Library Chowk was surprising,” he said. Residents said that this was the fifth leopard sighting since the last week of October. A female leopard was spotted on the Woodstock School road in October and days later, a giant leopard was caught on three CCTV cameras on Camel’s Back Road. There were two more sightings on Spring Road.
 Meanwhile, officials from the forest department have instructed people not to step out alone at night and have also promised increased vigil. “This was a rare incident. The cub had lost its way from the jungle and come to the town. The mother came looking for the cub and was aggressive for the same reason,” said forest officer Hoshiyar Singh Pundir.
 Local residents said that the sightings seem to have increased over the years. Khushpal Singh Chandok, a local resident said, “Many of us have seen leopards in quieter parts of the hill town in peak winter. This time of the year is a little too early for them to be around.”","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2033 chars)"
Fourth human kill by Corbett tiger in 2 months - The Times of India,2023-12-08,Uttarakhand,,Tiger,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Fourth human killed by a tiger in Corbett in two months.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"NAINITAL: A 32-year-old woman of Patrani Kargil village near Nainital was mauled to death by a tiger in Corbett Tiger Reserve (CTR) on Wednesday, marking the fourth human fatality since October 18 in the reserve. Earlier, three workers in the reserve were killed by prowling cats in Bijrani and Paterpani areas in November and October respectively.
This year, Uttarakhand has had at least 9 human lives lost to tiger attacks, with four occurring in CTR alone. While four people were left brutally injured in the attacks in Corbett, nine tigers have also died this year in the reserve owing to multiple reasons ranging from agerelated ailments to territorial fights. “In the past six months, our staff has con- ducted workshops sensitising around 2000 people in and around Corbett about the increasing tiger population. Despite this, villagers often neglect the importance of taking precautions.
In the recent incident, the woman had ventured almost a kilometer inside the forests to collect wood,” said Dheeraj Pandey, director of CTR. He added that “a drone team and camera traps have been deployed to identify the tiger.”
According to officials, the victim of the recent attack, Anita Devi (32) had gone into the forest with two other wo- men to collect wood when a prowling tiger attacked Anita. Frightened, the other two women ran away and informed forest officials about the entire episode. After combing the area, they found Anita’s body around 1 km inside the forest.
According to wildlife conservationists, the rising cases of human-wildlife conflict are mainly due to rising wildlife population, fragmentation of habitat, encroached wildlife corridors and urbanisation of fringe forests area.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1701 chars)"
"Angry tiger attacks tourist vehicle at Uttarakhand's Jim Corbett National Park, viral video terrifies internet - DNA India",2023-04-27,Uttarakhand,,Tiger,Human Injury,0.0,0.0,0.0,Tiger attacks tourist vehicle in Jim Corbett National Park.,0.98,single_incident,,DNA India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"VIRAL
Susanta Nanda, an Indian Forest Service officer, shared the video that shows an angry tiger charging toward a tourist vehicle at Jim Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand.
New Delhi: Nothing captures the human-wildlife conflict better than this footage of a tiger frightening a group of people on a safari adventure. We've seen several videos of how selfish humans act when they're in wildlife territory. Susanta Nanda, an Indian Forest Service officer, shared the video that shows an angry tiger charging toward a tourist vehicle at Jim Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand.
Watch it here:
	
Striped monk gets irritated 
		What will you do if at every designated hours people crash into your house as their matter of right? pic.twitter.com/4RDCVLWiRR
An enraged tiger can be seen growling and frightening the tourists inside the car in the video. As the tiger approached, the tourists became concerned and ordered the driver to reverse the car. You can hear the uproar that erupted as the enraged tiger tried to attack on the vehicle.
	“Striped monk gets irritated. What will you do if at every designated hours people crash into your house as their matter of right?”  Nanda captioned the clip on Twitter.
The clip went crazy viral on social and it has garnered mixed reactions as some called it “intrusive” and “oppressive” Check out a few reactions here:
An individual wrote, ""This seems to be becoming the norm now. Wonder if some introduction classes about appropriate behaviour in tourism/reserve forests would make a difference?"" Another added, ""the safari numbers needs to be curtailed - daily basis and also some weeks of no safari !"" ""It's unfortunate that we the people do not allow animals to live in forest nor outside forest.,"" posted a third. A fourth expressed, ""It's always tricky to let humans invade in jungles in any form or under any purpose. The nature ultimately has to pay the price."" What do you think about this video?","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1949 chars)"
Leopard Mauls Toddler To Death In North Kashmir’s Handwara - Kashmir Observer,2024-12-06,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Leopard mauls toddler to death.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Observer,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMingFBVV95cUxOVXVfV3J2ekxadEFFN0g2bkQ0bXdqejBmcUVrek4za3k0ckVmWFY2ME5ReWk4SDdHa29OQXhwcUw1amQxY3g1MUo3WEVrNXQwclo0d2NyaUFuczF1ZEdDMTlYVmlORnpPc3JBUjcwX0JWLVdoemZza2t0TnIyVW5PNWxkVmhwMGdqRTZmaTQxdlZCZDZFNHNvR0JEZHZkQQ?oc=5,,,"Representational Photo

