4 injured in leopard attack in Ganderbal - Greater Kashmir,2024-04-04,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,4.0,0.0,Injury reported in leopard attack.,0.98,single_incident,,Greater Kashmir,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Ganderbal, Apr 3: Two officials of the Wildlife Department and two women were among four persons injured in a leopard attack in the Fatehpora area of Ganderbal district on Wednesday.
Locals said that a leopard was seen roaming in the area on Wednesday morning, causing panic among the residents.
They said that they informed the Wildlife Department officials at the earliest.
Locals said that the leopard attacked the two Wildlife Department officials as they were laying a trap to capture the wild cat.
Two women were also injured in the leopard attack.
All four injured were shifted to District Hospital Ganderbal for medical aid.
After strenuous efforts, the animal was captured alive by the rescue teams of the Wildlife Department, Police, and Forest Protection Force.
The Wildlife authorities have advised people to take precautions while venturing out during the evening and inform the Wildlife Department about the presence of any wild animals.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 951 chars)"
Six Sheep Killed in Leopard Attack in Boniyar - Kashmir Life,2026-09-05,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Livestock Attack,6.0,0.0,0.0,Leopard killed six sheep in Boniyar.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigwFBVV95cUxNUVpNd0dzZUtmbUpfY20yNDZVbUEtazVqTnhOWENlQkpXMVNtVEpZaXZzTFI1Q19QdnhVSDVSVXVHSkdONkRNM1B6MmRabUtMNU8xWnU4cGVrT1NIVFNOUnhCWERPZ2hGOU1nS3NlQVFvWm13QmxvdlhUVTlSd0EwSVY0Zw?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: At least Six sheep were killed after a leopard entered a livestock enclosure at Barnate in Boniyar during the night, triggering concern among local residents.

According to reports, the leopard entered the enclosure and attacked the livestock, killing six sheep.

Following the incident, officials from the Wildlife Department visited the site and assessed the situation.

Residents have expressed concern over the leopard’s presence, particularly as a school with more than 300 students and a residential area are located only a few metres from the attack site.

“We are worried about the safety of our children and families,” residents said, urging the Wildlife Department to take immediate measures to capture and cage the leopard to prevent any further untoward incident.","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 785 chars)"
Leopard Killed After Injuring Six in JK’s Budgam - Asian Mail,2024-05-15,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,6.0,0.0,A leopard was killed after injuring humans in Budgam.,0.98,single_incident,,Asian Mail,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihwFBVV95cUxOOFRUeGtsbllBemNVUmxGV09PWlhkOW90T083TzlvVU93ZkNmSV9acUpJdU1BNkNHS0NXMXNkODNnZmdCYTRUT0wtT1Nkdm1KTHV4VGFtT3prLWU4cGlXSmd1dklaTGtPQkZKaEIwdmFfejZQRDlBd3NRRFNYSlNEV3hqT0tKOGs?oc=5,,,"Budgam: A leopard was killed after it attacked and injured six persons in central Kashmir’s Budgam district on Tuesday.

An official said that a wild leopard attacked and injured six persons at Nasrullah Pora while they were working in the agricultural land.

After the attack, the locals, however, rushed to the spot and immediately took all the injured to a nearby hospital where doctors referred Danish Ahmad Malik, among others, to SMHS Hospital for advanced treatment.

A doctor at the hospital confirmed the incident and identified all injured as Abdul Gaffar Rather aged 70 years, son of Abdul Samad; Adil Shafi Mir aged about 25 years, son of Mohmmad Shafi; Jahangir Ahmad Mir aged about 27 years, son of Mohmmad Shafi; Waqar Rashid Dar aged 21 years, son of Abdul Rashid; and Suhail Ahmad Magloo aged about 18 years, son of Bashir Ahmad, all residents of Nasrullahpora, Budgam.

A wildlife official said that the leopard was killed and has also been taken from the spot.","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 979 chars)"
Himachal youth survives leopard attack after daring 12-minute struggle in Solan village - The Tribune,2026-03-12,Himachal Pradesh,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A youth survived a leopard attack after a struggle.,0.95,single_incident,,The Tribune,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Himachal youth survives leopard attack after daring 12-minute struggle in Solan village
ITI student overpowers animal that attacked him in fields near Arki; suffers minor injuries
Leopards are rarely killed by humans and such incidents usually occur during man-animal conflict when the big cats are attacked in retaliation for targeting livestock or humans. In a rare incident, a youth in Sarli village of Arki subdivision in Solan district fought off a leopard that attacked him and killed the animal after a prolonged struggle on Monday morning.
The incident occurred around 7 am when Pravesh Sharma was crossing his fields to fetch milk. A leopard, which was lurking in the fields, suddenly attacked him.
Despite the sudden assault, Pravesh did not lose his presence of mind and grappled with the animal for nearly 10 to 12 minutes. During the fierce struggle, he managed to grab the leopard by its jaws and eventually overpowered the animal, killing it by smashing its neck. The leopard was reportedly a year-old female.
Pravesh, a student of the Industrial Training Institute (ITI) in Arki, sustained multiple scratches on his body during the struggle but did not suffer any serious injuries.
His uncle, Shyam Lal, said leopard movement had been reported in the area earlier on March 6 and villagers had informed the Forest Department about it. Residents had also requested the authorities to install a cage to capture the animal. Locals further claimed that the leopard had attempted to attack a scooter rider around 7.30 am on Monday, forcing him to abandon his vehicle and flee.
Forest Divisional Officer Rajkumar Sharma visited the spot and provided immediate relief of Rs 5,000 to the injured youth. Rajkumar said increasing human intrusion and shrinking natural habitats were forcing wild animals to stray into human settlements.
“Leopards generally attack people and livestock at night. This incident occurred early in the morning, reflecting the desperation of the young animal,” he said. He added that forest officials were working to create awareness among villagers so that wild animals could be safely driven back into forest areas. Capturing a leopard by installing cages, he said, is a technically complex task that requires trained experts.
As news of Pravesh’s bravery spread in the area, residents praised the youth for his courage. Teachers and students of his alma mater, ITI Arki, also enquired about his wellbeing after learning about the incident.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2474 chars)"
Leopard scare in Dehradun colonies after attack on 12-year-old boy - The Times of India,2024-01-16,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A boy was attacked by a leopard in Dehradun.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"DEHRADUN: On the day a 12-year-old boy was injured in a leopard attack in Dehradun, another leopard was spotted near the Forest Research Institute (FRI) in the heart of the city on Chakrata Road. Following this, FRI issued notices urging people to stick to the main roads within the campus and avoid unnecessary ventures after dark. The forest department has also heightened alertness at the campus.
On Sunday evening, Nikhil Thapa, 12, was attacked by a leopard while returning home with friends after collecting firewood near the Rispana riverbed in the Sundhowali area at Canal Road near the IT Park.
Nikhil's friends, Gautam and Umesh, acted quickly, shouting and pulling him away as the leopard attacked, preventing more severe injuries. Bleeding profusely, Nikhil was rushed to Government Doon Medical College Hospital, where he is set to undergo surgery.
Nikhil's father Sher Singh Thapa said, ""He will soon undergo scalp reconstructive surgery; he is okay and under observation."" He added that the family has received initial compensation of Rs 20,000.Early on Monday morning, the big cat was spotted in CCTV cameras in the Vrindanavan Enclave colony of the area.
Mussorie DFO Vaibhav Kumar under whose jurisdiction the area falls, said,""We had already alerted residents of nearby societies like Doon Trafalgar to be cautious. The Rispana river area, with bushes and vegetation, provides good habitat for leopards. In urban areas, leopards tend to hide during the day and hunt at night near habitation.""
To monitor the area, two cages, eight camera traps, and 20 personnel have been deployed, the DFO added. Notably, on December 26, a four-year-old boy was fatally attacked by a leopard in Singli village on Doon's fringes. The big cat remains uncaptured.
Meanwhile, chief minister Pushkar Singh Dhami expressed concern over increasing incidents of leopard attacks in the state. He directed principal secretary (forest) R K Sudhanshu to work on an action plan to stop such incidents. Dhami said strict action should be taken against the forest department officials who are negligent in preventing such incidents. 
The CM said that in cases of death due to human-wildlife conflict, a proposal should be brought soon to increase the ex-gratia amount provided as financial assistance to the family members of the deceased from Rs 4 lakh to Rs 6 lakh. He said new wildlife rescue centres should also be built in the state.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2426 chars)"
"Leopard kills youth in Mandi, injures eight - The Tribune",2025-12-25,Himachal Pradesh,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,8.0,1.0,A leopard killed a youth and injured others in Mandi.,0.98,single_incident,,The Tribune,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Panic gripped the Balh valley in Mandi district on Wednesday morning after a leopard attacked residents of three villages, killing one person and injuring eight others.
The reign of terror ended when alert villagers united and killed the leopard. The leopard first attacked residents in Chandyal village during the early hours, creating chaos and fear. It then moved to Bhadyal village, where it again attacked people indiscriminately. Four persons sustained serious injuries in these two villages. The leopard later reached Malwana village, where it fatally attacked a youth.
According to eyewitnesses, the youth was reportedly living in Mandi city and had come to the Balh valley to visit relatives. The deceased, identified as Balbir Singh, a native of Bheuli, died on the spot.
The injured include Sahib Singh, Deenanath, Champa Devi, Janit and Rekha Devi. The injured were rushed to Shri Lal Bahadur Shastri Medical College, Nerchowk, where they are undergoing treatment.
Following the attack, panic spread across the Balh valley. Residents came out of their homes carrying sticks and other improvised weapons to protect themselves. When the leopard attempted another attack, the villagers killed the animal. The leopard’s body has been sent for a post-mortem examination.
Teams from the administration, police and the Forest Department rushed to the spot to take stock of the situation.
Balh SDM Smritika Negi confirmed the incident and said immediate relief was being provided to the deceased’s family, while all possible assistance was being extended to the injured. All victims are out of danger. After an autopsy, the body of the deceased was handed over to his relatives for the last rites.
Mandi Divisional Forest Officer Vasu Doegar said a forest team immediately visited the spot and found the male leopard dead. It seemed the animal lost its territory and entered human habitation, he said.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1905 chars)"
Villagers in Uttarakhand hold forest officials hostage after series of leopard attacks on livestock - newindianexpress.com,2026-01-02,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,0.0,0.0,Villagers took action after multiple leopard attacks on livestock.,0.98,single_incident,,newindianexpress.com,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"DEHRADUN: In a dramatic turn of events, villagers in Mehalchouri and Kunigad areas of Gairsain in Chamoli district, Uttarakhand, took forest officials hostage for over two hours on Thursday night.
The villagers' patience snapped after a leopard killed a cow and its calf in the Ujetiya village, bringing the total number of livestock killed in the past one month to several. The villagers had been demanding the installation of a cage to capture the leopard, which has been on a rampage, attacking livestock in the area.
The latest incident sparked outrage among the villagers, who detained forest officials, demanding immediate action to capture the big cat. The villagers released the officials after a two-hour standoff, but not before warning of further agitation if their demands are not met.
""We have reached our breaking point,"" said a resident, requesting anonymity. ""If the leopard is targeting our cattle so openly, how long before it turns its attention to our children and the elderly?""
The affected zone lies near the sensitive border between the Garhwal and Kumaon regions. This single leopard, or possibly more, has caused widespread tension in the areas.
In Bhandarikod (Garhwal side), the predator reportedly killed three cows belonging to Krishna Nand Thapliyal. Earlier the same day, an attack was reported on Mohan Singh’s pet dog in Ujetiya, leaving it injured.
The carnage extends across the administrative boundary into Almora district (Kumaon side). Villagers in Pasara Gaon, Purana-lohba, and Nawan have reported five more cattle kills attributed to the leopard.
Driven by desperation, the enraged villagers of Ujetiya detained the on-site forest staff to demand immediate action. The standoff was resolved only after District Panchayat Member Suresh Bisht arrived and assured the community that traps would be installed immediately. Following the release of the officials, the villagers proceeded to bury the remains of the slain animals after post-mortem formalities.
Bisht, expressing grave concern, suggested the leopard might be turning man-eater due to the severity and frequency of the attacks. ""I have been in discussions with the DFO at the district headquarters,"" Bisht confirmed. ""Following talks with the Conservator, permission has now been granted to set up cages in the affected area.""
Local leaders, including Gram Pradhan Deepa Devi of Silanga and Block Panchayat Member Virendra Negi, demanded swift action, citing rapid success in Almora where traps were placed just two days after incidents there.
Divisional Forest Officer Pradeep Gaur confirmed the installation of cages and an increase in forest patrol frequency. ""We have deployed the necessary equipment and increased vigilance,"" Gaur stated, appealing to the public to remain cautious.
Meanwhile, sources within the Forest Department revealed that Uttarakhand lost 12 people to leopard attacks and seven to tiger attacks in 2024.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2930 chars)"
"Uttarakhand: 15 killed by big cats this year, max in leopard attacks - The Times of India",2024-07-21,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,15.0,"15 people have been killed by big cats, mainly leopards, in Uttarakhand this year.",0.98,aggregate_period_total,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"DEHRADUN: Uttarakhand lost nine people to leopard attacks and six to tiger attacks in seven months this year. In the most recent casualty on Thursday evening, a 17-year-old boy was killed by a leopard in Mehad village in Devprayag while returning home after playing cricket.
""People in the area were enraged by the boy's death in such a manner although officials say they have trapped the killer leopard,"" said Hoshiyar Singh, pradhan of Mehad village.
Forest authorities captured an adult male leopard early Friday morning from the same spot in Devprayag after an overnight search operation. The leopard is being sent to a rescue centre for medical examination, and its DNA samples will be matched with samples taken from the victim's body by scientists at the Wildlife Institute of India.
On Friday, a man was attacked by a leopard in a different part of the same forest division, in Kirtinagar area, and suffered leg injuries. The leopard was later found dead. According to the post-mortem report, it died from an existing maggot-inflicted wound.
Forest officials emphasise that awareness is key to preventing such attacks in a biodiversity-rich state like Uttarakhand. ""A scientific evaluation of human-wildlife conflicts over the past 23 years was conducted. Based on it, we are implementing short- and long-term interventions in 26 identified hotspots. These areas have been identified at both species and geographic levels, where forest landscape restoration and hi-tech equipment are being used to prevent encounters. As a result, we significantly reduced the number of deaths last year,"" said Samir Sinha, principal chief conservator of forests and chief wildlife warden of forest department.
Sinha added, ""While we are preparing thoroughly and equipping ourselves, it is important to realise we are dealing with animals whose behaviour we cannot change or predict. This inherent uncertainty remains, highlighting the crucial role of awareness in averting attacks. We are continuously spreading awareness and emphasising the importance of co-existence."" 
At a State Board for Wildlife meeting on Friday, authorities launched a poster campaign titled 'Coexistence - Don't be afraid, stay alert', which is being circulated across the state, especially among people residing close to forest areas.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2303 chars)"
"36 dead in wildlife attacks till June this yr, leopards top killers - The Times of India",2026-07-02,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,36.0,"36 deaths reported due to wildlife attacks this year, with leopards being the main culprits.",0.98,aggregate_period_total,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Dehradun:
 Uttarakhand has recorded 36 human deaths in wildlife attacks during the first six months of 2026—averaging six fatalities per month—with big cats accounting for the vast majority of the toll, according to recent forest department data.
Leopards were responsible for the highest number of fatalities at 15, followed closely by tigers at 11. The remaining deaths were attributed to elephants (5), crocodiles (3), and a bear and snake (1 each). With these latest casualties, the total number of human lives lost to human-wildlife conflict in Uttarakhand since its formation has reached 1,325.
Leopards also caused significant non-fatal injuries, with at least 46 people attacked by the animal in the same six-month period. For comparison, leopards claimed 18 lives and tigers claimed 12 in 2025, while 15 people were killed by leopards and 12 by tigers in 2024. Overall, 68 human lives were lost to wildlife attacks in 2025, down from 84 in 2024.
Given that tiger deaths (11) and leopard deaths (15) in just the first half of this year are already nearing previous annual figures, experts warn that total fatalities for 2026 may match or exceed last year’s annual totals.
Wildlife experts attribute the sustained pressure from tiger attacks to habitat saturation in and around the Corbett Tiger Reserve, which is pushing younger tigers into buffer zones and village fringes in search of territory.
Meanwhile, the high number of leopard cases reflects the predator’s extensive distribution across human-settled landscapes.
Joy Hukil, a veteran private wildlife shooter deployed during recent big cat tranquilising and elimination exercises, shared insights from his decade-long field experience. “The overpopulation of leopards and tigers is the key reason behind these escalating cases. Conservation efforts must extend beyond policy discussions and be backed by effective, science-based management on the ground,” Hukil said.
“We need accurate assessments of predator and prey populations, along with the availability of water, habitat and food resources across every landscape in the state. A balanced ecosystem and informed wildlife management can significantly reduce human-wildlife conflict,” Hukil said.
In response to the prevailing situation, the forest department has intensified operations in conflict hotspots. Measures currently being deployed include lemongrass plantations to deter wildlife, AI-based virtual walls, and the deployment of additional Quick Response Teams (QRTs) among others. The state has also announced the opening of rescue centers across all 13 districts, apart from special incentives to boost community awareness and engage locals in promoting human-wildlife coexistence.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2714 chars)"
17-year-old mauled by leopard in Uttarakhand's Tehri - The Times of India,2024-07-19,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A teenager was mauled by a leopard in Tehri.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"NEW TEHRI: A 17-year-old boy has been mauled by a leopard in Uttarakhand's Tehri district, officials said on Friday. The incident took place in Devprayag on Thursday night when Anurag, a class 12 student, was returning home after playing cricket and was attacked by the leopard, Kirtinagar sub-divisional magistrate Sonia Pant said. 
Despite some local people raising an alarm, the leopard did not leave the boy, the officials said. 
According to the officials, police and forest department personnel reached the spot after receiving the information of the incident. 
The victim's mutilated body was recovered late Thursday night, Pant said. 
BJP MLA from Devprayag Vinod Kandari, who reached the spot on Friday, directed the forest department to capture the leopard immediately. 
By 6 am, a team of the forest department successfully trapped the leopard in a cage, the officials said. 
However, the angry locals created a ruckus, demanding that the leopard be shot on the spot, they said. 
The forest department officials told them that the captured leopard would be taken only to the office in the tehsil, to which they agreed. 
Upon reaching the tehsil, when the local people discovered that the leopard was being transported to Dehradun via Hindolakhal, they threw out the employees of the ranger office and staged a sit-in, locking the place, the officials said. 
Divisional forest officer Jeevan Dagade calmed the people down and appealed to them to not let their children go out of homes alone in the evening.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1516 chars)"
Uttarakhand CM directs forest department to take preventive steps after leopard attacks children - ANI News,2024-01-15,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,0.0,0.0,Leopard attacks prompted preventive measures in Uttarakhand.,0.98,single_incident,,ANI News,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Dehradun (Uttarakhand) [India], January 15 (ANI): Expressing concern over leopards attacking children in several areas of Dehradun, Chief Minister Pushkar Singh Dhami directed Principal Secretary Forest RK Sudhanshu to work on an effective action plan to stop such incidents.
At a meeting in the Secretariat, the Chief Minister said that strict action should be taken against the Forest Department officials who are negligent in preventing such incidents.
To catch the leopards, cages should be installed and night patrolling should be done.
He also said that in areas where incidents of human-wildlife conflict are taking place, the forest department should be kept on 24-hour alert mode.
The Chief Minister said that in cases of death due to human-wildlife conflict, a proposal should be brought soon to increase the ex-gratia amount provided as financial assistance to the family members of the deceased from Rs 4 lakh to Rs 6 lakh.
CM Dhami said that new wildlife rescue centres should also be built. 
Earlier, Uttarakhand Chief Minister Pushkar Singh Dhami participated in a cleanliness drive at Kainchi Dham in Nainital on Sunday as part of the Bharatiya Janata Party's (BJP) campaign at religious places ahead of Ayodhya's Ram temple inauguration.
Chief Minister Pushkar Singh Dhami said, ""Our Lord Ram is going to be seated at his birthplace on January 22. The entire country is filled with joy due to the fortnight of cleanliness, Deepotsav and Ram Bhajan. Everybody is excited and happy that the wait for the Pran Pratishtha of Ram Lalla will finally be over.""
Highlighting the cleanliness campaign started in Uttarakhand, Chief Minister Pushkar Singh Dhami said, ""We are starting the cleanliness campaign from Baba Neem Karoli Dham today. This drive will be run at all the religious public places in the state."" (ANI)","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1828 chars)"
"6 kills and counting, leopards terrorise Udaipur villages; Army, drones in race against time - The Indian Express",2024-09-30,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,6.0,Six people killed by leopards in Udaipur villages.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,,The Indian Express,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxPMWtMeDc3akxrTUdwZi0zQldHRlBqRWs5MXdheF9CYXYwWTB0UTJQcXVYN25yckRZaWdEcEZfaURhVmN2eFdQNDNDNF9qcE9teHdMdjVERjdESUhEUUtsUDR5Y2Y4QTNINjNBSUdYVjB1RmIwZGlReUg3eEZURV8yTzBTSkk4T2xZTGFWV1pVaVJfZGJ2cTVRTTFsQ1lfb1JXMTQ1aFhrQ01ickF0c2pBUGdPWHA1Z2R60gG_AUFVX3lxTFA0MmlOOS1xVUxrT0VDSkpEUVBla2JVTXdONUxmYjBBdEtNSUZadjBOYjV1ZndyWS1hNWVVMFBVUHJpbk8zZ1JEYnVlZWxidkJRNjMwdGdCUk9OTWxtV19KUEN6WU82WGIzRWEzTDE3cy1VS3BaVG9taUtPWFYwaUh1NUowbFlLdkxMT1ZqdE5jZlZRUlpoYWdSS2xFOXJEczFaekJUZFRNeERpMHlyc2pNeVdTTmVkSTh1eTVkMHBV?oc=5,,,"6 kills and counting, leopards terrorise Udaipur villages; Army, drones in race against time
The latest victim, 60-year-old Gattu Bai, had gone to graze goats near Majawad village on Saturday morning. When she didn't return home by nightfall, her family alerted forest officials, who found her mangled remains some 300 metres from her home.
When their cows returned home alone at 7 pm on September 18, Keshav Bhil (21) knew something was wrong. His 16-year-old sister, Kamala, who had gone out to graze them in the forest that morning, was meant to return home by 4 pm.
When she wasn’t seen even by nightfall, village residents set out with torches to look for her.
“Early morning, around 80 men from the village searched for her in the forest and finally found her mutilated body deep in the forest,” Keshav says, his eyes welling up.
Kamala is among 10 people who have been killed in leopard attacks in the contiguous forest areas of Udaipur and Rajsamand since May. Six of them, including a five-year-old girl, were killed between September 19 and 30.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1054 chars)"
Leopard Beats a Hasty Retreat after Dogs Attack in Haridwar - ETV Bharat,2024-06-01,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,0.0,0.0,Leopard retreated after being attacked by dogs in Haridwar.,0.98,single_incident,,ETV Bharat,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Leopard Beats a Hasty Retreat after Dogs Attack in Haridwar
Leopards attacking human beings and hunting animals is quite normal, but have you ever witnessed dogs taking on a leopard and in the process, the leopard beating a hasty retreat? If not, can watch a video where dogs attack the leopard in the Bahadarabad area of Uttarakhand.
Haridwar (Uttarakhand): You must have witnessed a leopard hunting a dog, but in the video, the dogs surrounded the leopard that came to hunt the dogs. A leopard ventured out of the forest and entered a dairy farm in the Bahadarabad area of Haridwar on Friday.
The leopard came to hunt the dogs. But, the dogs are not scared of the leopard. They showed courage and faced the leopard bravely. The dogs overpowered the leopard so much that they injured it.
Meanwhile, after hearing the barking of dogs, the owner of the dairy farm rushed to the spot and was surprised to see the fight of dogs with the leopard. He hurriedly closed the door of the room. In this way, the leopard got trapped in the room. The leopard got so scared of the attack of the dogs that it went and sat on top of the cupboard. The forest department was informed about the presence of the leopard. The team of the forest department reached the spot to rescue the leopard.
Haridwar's Forest Range Officer Shailendra Negi said that information was received about a leopard being trapped in the Bahadarabad area at 3 am on Friday. After which, when we reached the spot, the leopard was found locked in a room. Later, a rescue operation was launched to rescue the leopard.
Initially, an attempt was made to tranquillize the leopard twice, but for some reason, it was not tranquillized. After that, the leopard was caught directly in the cage. He said that the leopard came out of the forest to the area to hunt a dog, but it got trapped. At present, the leopard has been sent to the rescue centre for first aid.
Read more: Leopard Attacks Dog In Goregaon, Captured On CCTV","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1972 chars)"
Terrifying VIDEO: Man Kills Leopard With Bare Hands In Uttarakhands Tehri - Free Press Journal,2024-07-21,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,0.0,0.0,A man killed a leopard in Tehri.,0.98,single_incident,,Free Press Journal,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"In a terrifying viral video, a leopard attacked was seen attacking a 56-year old man in Uttrakhand's Jakhi village of Kirtinagar block, Devprayag assembly constituency. In the video the man can be seen severely injured and the leopard was seen dead on a floor.
According to tweet posted by @AjitSinghRathi, the man in the video Lakshman Singh Negi attacked the already injured leopard around its throat. The tweet also mentioned that, the leg of the leopard was badly injured, the wire was stuck in its neck, the leopard died shortly after falling. In the video the Lakshman Singh Negi's hand can been seen brutally injured.
According to media report in hindi news portal Hindustanlive, Laxman Singh Negi was walking from his house in Jakhi to Khumkhu when the leopard, hiding in bushes 50 meters below the old Jakhi road, launched the attack on him. Villagers quickly alerted the forest department and Devprayag MLA Vinod Kandhari. The forest department team, upon arriving at the scene, discovered the dead leopard nearby. The department conducted a postmortem on the leopard to determine the exact cause of death, as the animal appeared to have sustained previous injuries, likely from being trapped in an iron net.
According to other media reports, Kirtinagar Range Ranger Buddhi Prakash confirmed that the leopard had old wounds on its neck from an iron net, which may have driven it to attack a human. The forest department has completed the postmortem, and the exact cause of the leopard's death will be known once the report is available.
Meanwhile, the forest department is preparing to provide compensation for Laxman Singh Negi's injuries. The community hopes that such incidents can be minimized in the future to ensure safety for both humans and wildlife.
In a recent incident reported from Uttrakhand, a 17-year-old boy named Anurag was mauled by a leopard in Devprayag, Tehri district, on Thursday night. Anurag, a class 12 student, was returning home after playing cricket when the leopard attacked him. Despite local residents raising an alarm, the leopard did not release the boy, officials reported on Friday.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2128 chars)"
