"Leopard kills 5-yr-old girl in Shirur village, 3rd such death in Pune district since April - The Times of India",2025-10-13,,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Leopard killed a 5-year-old girl in Shirur village.,0.98,single_incident,,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Pune: A leopard hiding in a sugar cane field killed a five-year-old girl who was on her way to give water to her grandfather working in a farm 300 metres from their house at 10am in Pimparkhed village at Shirur taluka, about 75 km from Pune, on Sunday.
The girl, Shivanya Nilesh Bombe, sustained severe injuries to her neck and hand. She was rushed to the Manchar Civil Hospital, where doctors declared her dead on arrival.
Her uncle, Ganesh Bombe, told reporters: ""Shivanya's grandfather, Arun, witnessed the attack. He tried to save her, but the leopard had already inflicted serious injuries before disappearing into the fields.""
The girl's parents are farmers. They have another daughter younger to Shivanya. Family members said they had spotted leopards in the area before and their dog was attacked around two weeks ago.
This was the second death in a leopard attack in Shirur and the third in Pune district, this year.
Earlier, on Sept 24, a six-year-old boy studying outside his house was killed by a leopard in Chincholi village, Junnar. In April, an 85-year-old woman sleeping outdoors because of the scorching heat was mauled to death in Shirur's Inamgaon. 
That apart, two people were injured in leopard attacks in the district, this year.
In the fiscal year 2024-25, Pune district had reported nine deaths and nine injuries in leopard attacks.
A senior forest official said leopards frequent Pimparkhed village and its surroundings. At least three deaths in leopard attacks have been reported in Pimparkhed and nearby Jambut village in the past. ""We have set up a base camp near Pimparkhed for swift response. Additionally, three cages have been installed in the area to capture the big cats,"" forest assistant conservator Smita Rajhans told TOI.
Meanwhile, the area where the incident occurred on Sunday is surrounded by sugar cane fields, making it prone to leopard activity. Rajhans said forest authorities would install 10 more cages and trap cameras in the village to catch the big cat.
Former Lok Sabha MP of Shirur Shivajirao Adhalrao Patil visited the hospital to meet the family. He said leopard attacks are frequent in the region and demanded a long-term solution. ""Sterilisation of leopards is the only viable solution, but no concrete steps have been taken so far,"" he added.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2300 chars)"
"Five-year-old boy, elderly woman killed in separate leopard attacks in Uttarakhand - newindianexpress.com",2026-02-04,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,2.0,Two human casualties reported from leopard attacks in Uttarakhand.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,,newindianexpress.com,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"RUDRAPRAYAG/NAINITAL: A 5-year-old boy and an elderly woman were killed in separate leopard attack incidents in Uttarakhand, police said on Wednesday.
In Sindravani village of Rudraprayag district, a leopard snatched a child playing in his courtyard on Tuesday afternoon and dragged him into the forest.
Upon receiving the information, teams from the district administration and the forest department reached the spot and launched a search for the child.
Rudraprayag Sub-Divisional Magistrate Sohan Singh Saini, who led the search operation, said that seven teams conducted a continuous search, after which the child's mutilated body was recovered from the forest at 11 pm.
The child was identified as Daksh Bisht, and the body was sent for post-mortem.
Saini said that in view of the potential threat from the leopard, cages will be set up at various locations and a forest department team will continuously patrol the area.
Meanwhile, the Rudraprayag district administration has declared a holiday for the next two days in eight schools in the area for the safety of the children.
In another incident, a 60-year-old woman was killed in a leopard attack in the Juna Estate area of Bhimtal in Nainital district.
Officials said that Ganga Devi had gone to the forest to collect fodder for her cattle on Monday, but did not return till late evening.
Following this, the villagers started searching for her.
The woman's body was recovered from deep inside the forest on Tuesday morning, bearing clear signs of a leopard attack, officials said.
The villagers alleged that leopard activity has been observed in the area for quite some time, but the forest department has not yet taken any concrete steps to catch it.
They demanded that cages be set up in the area.
Nathuwakhan Forest Range Officer Vijay Bhatt said that as soon as the information about the incident was received, teams from the forest department and the police reached the spot.
He said that patrolling has been intensified in the area and efforts are being made to catch the leopard.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 2046 chars)"
Forest Dept Shooter Kills 'Man-Eating' Leopard In Uttarakhand Village - ETV Bharat,2025-12-11,Uttarakhand,,Leopard,Retaliation Against Wildlife,0.0,0.0,0.0,A leopard was killed by forest department as a retaliation measure in Uttarakhand.,0.95,single_incident,,ETV Bharat,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxPWXEzUHV3aWt4QXZpMHZMRjdxR2RieHA2VjVQMmNBb3lLc3VpSDVGV0l5NjhOOGRWTk54SGxxWGh1bl9QNnh3V0dVQ2VKSG0tZTJ2Z19TeHpjaDNYVXRGM2REZ21mejNjLW5HS2xMNHFoYlNWdThZSk1FY3Z1N3BOeEVDME40OTJSdHdTbHppbEtBcWhOaEVsUldNbk42VzF5bWtsZUFpR09GRV94STRDMXpkd3VSQTVT0gG-AUFVX3lxTFBYRmE2bXRkWWtMVkRCTV9WUUMyY3JaY3JRcEJaYjYxZ0kxZm5IcVRIcW4yTWVpclV1c2F4elRYVXZNSGZKTTJoVXBjelNtdW5EZmpJdndWMkJWZ1NvYzIxOHk3RGtRSkwxZ0p1Qlc4ZmJxckNVRkxlTVFmTGtfOVNBbkNwS2VxWmJEN255WVdiNUI0dnYxUF9DNXpmMl8zV1loRTk1Y1llWDBGTmpBYjQyZFJRUWV6aFgxdjM4RHc?oc=5,,,"Forest Dept Shooter Kills 'Man-Eating' Leopard In Uttarakhand Village
The leopard was killed by shooter Joy Hukil and team giving a sigh of relief to villagers of Gajald who had been living in fear.
Pauri: Locals at Gajald village here on Wednesday heaved a sigh of relief after a shooter hired by the Forest Department shot dead a 'man-eating' leopard.
Pauri Forest Divisional Officer, Abhimanyu Singh confirmed that the female leopard around five years old, was killed by shooter Joy Hukil and his team on Wednesday night. The killed leopard is the same captured in the trap cameras installed in the area, Singh said. The leopard's carcass was later transported to the Nagdev Forest Range in Pauri for further proceedings. Singh said that a DNA sample of the killed leopard will be taken and tested to ascertain if it was the same leopard.
Villagers had been living under fear due to the terror caused by the leopard. Last Thursday, a 45-year-old man, Rajendra Nautiyal, was attacked and killed by a leopard at the village. Since then, leopard activity has been continuously observed in the area. On Wednesday, a leopard attacked a woman in the Chaubattakhal area leaving her grievously injured. Several domestic animals were also attacked by the big cat, causing widespread anger among the villagers. The villagers demanded that, in addition to the departmental shooters, a private shooter be immediately deployed to eliminate the man-eating leopard.
Amid the outrage, Chief Minister Pushkar Singh Dhami had directed the Forest Department to take action in this regard. The department hired the shooter and neutralised the big cat in a coordinated effort.
Following the twin attacks in the area, a team of senior officials led by Principal Secretary (Forest) R K Sudhanshu had visited the spot and met the affected villagers. (With agency inputs)
