Two Persons Critically Injured in Leopard Attack in North Kashmir - Kashmir Life,2026-04-12,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,2.0,0.0,Two persons were critically injured in a leopard attack.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMingFBVV95cUxPY1NMaW5kNlFqX3BPQTZKb1YzUjkyMWlMdm5laFhDb1NVNEpCLUlwZmphd1NCUUM2OXVTTnBCb0QyN2hZSWRra3hfdFY0c0t6NjhfYzZmUk9KaFMwSkdLSTdfXzUweGhWeFFqcFJwbWctUjlWOEY4YU90UktsY1VkbEYtNnpMOEZYamxWMmUxbkFkdzgyYmZjV1Nndi1SQQ?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: Two persons were seriously injured in a leopard attack in Tangmarg area of north Kashmir’s Baramulla district on Sunday, officials said.

The injured have been identified as Abdul Rahim Ganai and Mohammad Sultan Najar.

According to locals, the leopard had been roaming in the Marchipora and Chandilora areas of Tangmarg for the past several days, triggering panic among the public.

Eyewitnesses said the leopard first attacked Najar near Gousia Model School in Chandilora and soon after attacked Ganai in Mirchipora village. Both were critically injured in the incidents.

The injured were shifted to the Sub-District Hospital (SDH) Tangmarg, where they are undergoing treatment.

Meanwhile, residents have urged the Wildlife Department to take immediate action and capture the leopard to prevent further attacks. (KNS)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 831 chars)"
"16 Sheep Killed, 17 Injured in Leopard Attack in Central Kashmir - Kashmir Life",2026-09-10,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Livestock Attack,16.0,0.0,0.0,"Leopard attacked livestock, resulting in 16 sheep killed and 17 injured.",0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimwFBVV95cUxNcDJCcVhtTXpybTdNMzJhZXA3ZkRTM1ZVdHdWSVFCRTg1eV9rRzlMVWgzb3BwTDhGOFVCdmNwelpoSmtvM3dOME5iOEZPLWpSRFNOblQ0bXUwV3hkSElJdzY1ck9lcDlCZElENHg2YkJVZGxrdlUwTlgwbDJiRUxTSlNFVDNYM2VyZUNCZVFIV1RlNC1VeXF2Q0FvMA?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: Sixteen sheep were killed and 17 others injured after a leopard attacked a flock in the Chadoora area of central kashmir’s Budgam district during the intervening night of Wednesday and Thursday.

Imtiyaz Ahmad Chopan, owner of the flock said that he believed a leopard had attacked his sheep, though the animal’s identity is yet to be confirmed.

He said the animal entered the livestock enclosure in Badipora village of Chadoora during the night and attacked the flock. “We depend on livestock for our livelihood, and the incident has caused significant financial losses for us,” he said.

Chopan appealed to the government and concerned departments to provide immediate assistance and compensation for the loss of livestock.

Meanwhile, locals also urged the Wildlife Department to inspect the site and take necessary measures to prevent further attacks in the area.(KNO)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 883 chars)"
"16 sheep killed, 17 injured in leopard attack in Budgam - thekashmirhorizon.com",2026-09-10,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Livestock Attack,16.0,0.0,0.0,"A leopard attacked livestock in Budgam, leading to 16 sheep killed and 17 injured.",0.98,single_incident,,thekashmirhorizon.com,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinAFBVV95cUxQRTlLM0RlMkJsQnR4SGR5NDdDSldIQl9NaXhVQ1BsclBuT2pEQm9VZkExOHJFMjZUcUJpQjMzTGxERjBOUTJmN2ZUTV9pcjdSWmxTa0l1SFJUT01Xa2w5NjNvTkR0M044ZnFfV2tZZTZrSmxvV282dGlmQS1LUnZyaUdlc1Z4cTBMcDNodHhjZzlYRVVTTkpEX1htbnA?oc=5,,,"Budgam, Sep 10 (KNO): Sixteen sheep were killed and 17 others injured after a leopard attacked a flock in the Chadoora area of Budgam district during the intervening night of Wednesday and Thursday.

Imtiyaz Ahmad Chopan, owner of the flock, told the news agency—Kashmir News Observer (KNO) that he believed a leopard had attacked his sheep, though the animal’s identity is yet to be confirmed.

He said the animal entered the livestock enclosure in Badipora village of Chadoora during the night and attacked the flock. “We depend on livestock for our livelihood, and the incident has caused significant financial losses for us,” he said.

Chopan appealed to the government and concerned departments to provide immediate assistance and compensation for the loss of livestock.

Meanwhile, locals also urged the Wildlife Department to inspect the site and take necessary measures to prevent further attacks in the area.—(KNO)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 923 chars)"
"16 Sheep Killed, 17 Injured in Suspected Leopard Attack in Budgam - Kashmir Age",2026-09-10,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Livestock Attack,16.0,0.0,0.0,"In Budgam, a suspected leopard attack led to 16 sheep killed and 17 injured.",0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Age,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMioAFBVV95cUxQMHV3SjU0ak5ETEZlYlB2Sk1oZXo2TzhINVBYbzBBOFlnckNJTF9xeU1udW9XMXhNMmh5VkdVaGtQNllwMHZjUDZKb01NaTN0V1ZnQ1ZZVzl0UFVhaUNTbldPNDRaTDZBNnBjMXVvRUR5VXFlWnY1NV8ydlltTVpSd3ZOU1d6UWxfS3hIdGU0NFBjYVA1SWNxc19Qb0FnYzBW?oc=5,,,"Sixteen sheep were reportedly killed and 17 others injured after a wild animal attacked a flock at Badipora village in the Chadoora area of Budgam district during the intervening night of Wednesday and Thursday.
Imtiyaz Ahmad Chopan, owner of the flock, said he suspected that a leopard had entered the livestock enclosure during the night and attacked the sheep. However, the identity of the animal has not been independently confirmed.
Chopan said the incident had caused significant financial losses to his family, which depends on livestock for its livelihood. He appealed to the government and concerned departments to assess the damage and provide compensation and immediate assistance.
Locals have urged the Wildlife Department to inspect the area and take necessary measures to prevent further attacks on livestock and possible encounters between residents and the wild animal.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 885 chars)"
Leopard Attack at Budgam Brick Kiln Leaves UP Labourer Dead - Rising Kashmir,2026-06-05,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,A leopard attack in Budgam resulted in the death of a labourer.,0.98,single_incident,,Rising Kashmir,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijwFBVV95cUxNc2prV044RkxxQmVOYzUyUEUyZ0dVeV9fZDkyMjVzRi1vZkRybU8yX1RjX1cwWGEzdWFNd1JEenB1SmQ5c0xFWVFKSzRldHdRWTRHTTZrREJWTXBHVjI3UUpXUnhpemtTWVNxRXFBeVMyV1loTE5oanEwTnJYYmtMa2VKblJINGdCT0xIZkk0VQ?oc=5,,,"Srinagar, June 5: A 45-year-old migrant labourer from Uttar Pradesh was killed in a leopard attack in the Beerwah area of Budgam district on Friday, officials said.
The victim, identified as Harish Chander, son of Kashi and a resident of Uttar Pradesh, was employed at a brick kiln in Baput, Beerwah, owned by Mohd Altaf Thoker.
According to police, the labourer was attacked by a leopard near the brick kiln. His body was later discovered a short distance away from the worksite.
Upon receiving information about the incident, police personnel reached the scene and took custody of the body.
The deceased was subsequently shifted to a hospital for medico-legal procedures.
Police have initiated proceedings into the incident, while wildlife officials are expected to visit the area to assess the situation and implement necessary safety measures to prevent further attacks.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 874 chars)"
"Girl, 6, injured after Leopard enters house in Shopian - Asian Mail",2026-07-23,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A six-year-old girl was injured when a leopard entered her house.,0.98,single_incident,,Asian Mail,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijgFBVV95cUxQbWlwcWZzd0ZENVZ5VlJBOHVHQ21odEkyVTZUVUpMRkM3R1dfX3hpV3FmTGNiUUtKUTJMQkRZRTFKMF9odEFMUDFFQ2xsTzVFeWlWLUotZ1lLNlJZMUVQdnVKOEhCX2NIRFhfeThnYzFFRUlucjdsWWU0aWZBY0w0Y1hGUjFDZENUT0J2SVNB?oc=5,,,"Shopian: A six-year-old girl was injured after a leopard entered a residential house in the forest area of Laitri Wali Behak in the Keller area of south Kashmir’s Shopian district on Thursday.

An official told the news agency—Kashmir News Observer (KNO) that the leopard entered the residential house in Keller and attacked the minor girl, leaving her injured.

He said the injured girl was immediately shifted to District Hospital Shopian for treatment, where she is undergoing medical care.

The girl was later identified as Aafia Jan (6), daughter of Farooq Ahmad Tedwa, a resident of Zrakan Shalidar, Keller in Shopian.

Meanwhile, police have taken cognisance of the incident and initiated an investigation.—(KNO)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 719 chars)"
Leopard Attacks Minor Girl In South Kashmir - Kashmir Life,2026-08-04,Jammu & Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,A minor girl was injured in an attack by a leopard.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigAFBVV95cUxNc0VtOV9YaDFKZktSMzRyVjFZZTNrTVRFSmZtSTUtQVhmS3hFNkgwM3VnUGo3QUFVX216RFJCVDJmT21NSENXSHFPMzhncFR0WEtZWlEyM29tZzhPY2RoMXAyLWUxRUNoOWZHejdIY2F3bzc1UUtZY1BkMGdia1RkMg?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: A minor girl was injured after being attacked by a leopard in Dragad village of south Kashmir’s Shopian district on Tuesday.

Officials said the girl, identified as Zanab, daughter of Aafiq Ahmad Thoker, a resident of Dragad, was attacked by the leopard while she was in the village.

She sustained injuries in the attack and was immediately shifted to a nearby hospital for treatment. Her condition is stated to be stable, the officials said.

Following the incident, residents urged the Wildlife Department to take immediate measures to prevent such attacks and ensure the safety of people in the area.","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 614 chars)"
Labourer Mauled to Death in Leopard Attack in Central Kashmir - Kashmir Life,2026-06-05,Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,Labourer mauled to death by leopard in Central Kashmir.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxNeldpVUxtYTNZeWdpb0xMdFFhQVBuV2dZY0VfNzVpbjhLbU9FNlFhSEZKT3JLUkoweHYzbU5QOEVSbEF2Q2U1a1ZrZFo4MjB4WjkxNHlFS0lIbUFvdTRaeVlydUJOUktVY1hodVFRVFJ5V293cEVHWE9CWkZTU3dzNmM1V0NQSFhEVmtZYk5MNng5bEl0TnE5MQ?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: A non-local labourer was killed in a leopard attack in the Beerwah area of central Kashmir’s Budgam district on Friday, officials said.

The deceased has been identified as Harish Chander (45), son of Kashi, a resident of Uttar Pradesh. He was working at a brick kiln located at Baput, Beerwah, owned by Mohd Altaf Thoker.

A police official said that the labourer came under attack by a leopard, and his body was later recovered a short distance from the brick kiln.

A police team from Budgam reached the spot soon after receiving information and took possession of the body.

The body was shifted to hospital for medico-legal formalities.

Meanwhile, police have taken cognisance of the incident, while wildlife authorities are expected to assess the situation and take necessary measures in the area. (KDC)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 820 chars)"
12-Year-Old Boy Killed in Leopard Attack in North Kashmir - Kashmir Life,2026-06-09,Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Death,0.0,0.0,1.0,12-year-old boy killed in leopard attack in North Kashmir.,0.98,single_incident,,Kashmir Life,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikwFBVV95cUxOZXQtT21KNk9tZ1l0Q3BGREYxVUlOS2RhbHpUV3ozS1pXeVB4NTl3eE9sQjVNUHIzZFVMWmpHWnNmZzZPUHVIcGVabGVTR3BMbjFWRG9Ta3l1SF83bmxPV3NRVkpHOTZYTGg4aFNRRGRqSDlXY0g2WHdfWUhZbWlKNDRMel9HaGFIcUJ0OEdoZU1haWM?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: A 12-year-old boy was killed in a leopard attack in the remote village of Manzpathra, Chowkibal area of north Kashmir’s Kupwara district on Tuesday evening, triggering fear, grief and protests among local residents.

According to official sources, Tajamul Bashir, son of Bashir Ahmad Gujjar, was in the courtyard of his home at around 5:30 pm when a leopard suddenly appeared and attacked him. The animal reportedly dragged the child towards a nearby forest area before disappearing into the woods.

Following the incident, villagers launched a large-scale search operation. However, despite their efforts, the boy was later found dead, having sustained fatal injuries in the attack.

The incident has left the entire area in shock and mourning. Residents expressed deep concern over the increasing movement of wild animals near human habitations and staged protests against the Wildlife Department, accusing it of failing to take adequate preventive measures.

Locals said this was the second leopard-related incident in the area within the past month. They claimed that a child had been injured in a similar attack a few weeks earlier, after which the Wildlife Department installed a trap cage in the area. However, residents alleged that the cage failed to capture the leopard, allowing the threat to persist.

Protesters demanded immediate and effective action from the authorities, stating that the leopard poses a serious threat to the safety of local residents. They urged the administration and Wildlife Department to take urgent steps to capture the animal and prevent further loss of human life.

Meanwhile, the Tehsildar Kralpora, Naib Tehsildar Kralpora, and senior police officials visited the bereaved family to offer condolences and assured them of all possible assistance from the government.

The Divisional Forest Officer (DFO), Wildlife North Kashmir, said that an emergency operation has been launched to track and capture the leopard. He added that Wildlife Department teams have been deployed in the area and efforts are underway on a war footing to prevent any further incidents.

A senior Wildlife Department official also confirmed that field staff are actively searching for the animal and expressed hope that it would be captured soon.

The incident has triggered widespread concern across the region, with residents urging authorities to strengthen wildlife management measures and ensure the safety of people living in vulnerable forest-adjacent areas. (JKNS)","success(newspaper3k, resolved_via_decode, 2498 chars)"
Girl Injured in Leopard Attack in South Kashmir's Shopian - The Chenab Times,2026-07-23,Kashmir,,Leopard,Human Injury,0.0,1.0,0.0,"Girl injured after leopard attack in Shopian, South Kashmir.",0.98,single_incident,,The Chenab Times,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"SRINAGAR: A six-year-old girl sustained injuries after a leopard entered her family’s home in the Laitri Wali Behak forest area of Keller, Shopian district, in South Kashmir on Thursday.
The Chenab Times has learned that the wild animal attacked the minor girl shortly after entering the residential house. The injured child was promptly transported to the District Hospital Shopian for immediate medical attention and is currently receiving treatment.
The victim has been identified as Aafia Jan, the six-year-old daughter of Farooq Ahmad Tedwa, a resident of Zrakan Shalidar, Keller.
Following the incident, local police have registered a case and initiated an investigation into the circumstances surrounding the leopard’s entry into the house and the subsequent attack.
Wild animal incursions into residential areas have become a recurring concern in several parts of Jammu and Kashmir, particularly in regions bordering forests. These incidents often raise questions about wildlife management strategies and human-animal conflict mitigation efforts. The presence of dense forests in many districts provides a natural habitat for various wild animals, including leopards, which occasionally venture into human settlements in search of prey or due to habitat fragmentation.
Authorities have previously advised residents in fringe forest areas to exercise caution, particularly during dawn and dusk, and to take preventive measures to secure their homes. This includes ensuring that doors and windows are properly closed and that children and pets are supervised when outdoors. Local wildlife departments often conduct awareness programs to educate communities on coexisting with wildlife and reporting sightings or encounters to relevant authorities.
The incident in Shopian highlights the ongoing challenges faced by residents in managing the proximity of wildlife. The District Hospital Shopian is equipped to handle such emergencies, and officials stated that the girl’s condition is being closely monitored by medical professionals. The police investigation aims to ascertain the exact reasons behind the animal’s unusual behavior and its presence in a residential area.
