Mohan safari zone in Corbett shut after 2 tiger attacks in a month - The Times of India,2026-05-05,Uttarakhand,,Tiger,aggregate_period_total,0.0,0.0,0.0,Two tiger attacks occurred in the Mohan safari zone in Corbett within a month.,0.98,aggregate_period_total,unrecognized_conflict_type:aggregate_period_total,The Times of India,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Bageshwar:
 The Mohan safari zone in the Corbett Tiger Reserve, located in Tadam village of Talla Salt, has been shut indefinitely after two tiger attacks in a month, officials said on Tuesday. The zone allowed around 20 safari jeeps per day.
Mohan range officer Ganga Saran said, “Our focus is on identifying and capturing the animal. Patrolling is under way in nearby villages and experts from Corbett Tiger Reserve have been called in.”
The move follows protests by villagers, who said safari activity since its opening in 2024 has disturbed wildlife movement.
The latest incident occurred on Sunday evening when 53-year-old Mahipal Singh Mehra was killed by a tiger while cutting grass near his field. Earlier, an elderly villager was also killed in a similar attack in the same area.
Social activist Narayan Singh Rawat said rising tourism pressure was pushing animals out of their habitat. “While the govt earns revenue from tourism, residents are facing the consequences,” he added.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 989 chars)"
Two women fall to death after bear attack in Chamoli - Prashant News,2026-09-05,Uttarakhand,,Asiatic Black Bear,Human Death,0.0,0.0,2.0,"Two women died after a bear attack in Chamoli, Uttarakhand.",0.98,single_incident,,Prashant News,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiigFBVV95cUxQTkVJVFJnNGVnUFQtWXlfMGRFSm5Od25nYjh0aXpSVjlUeFlxRV9vSGk5MWZRbzBfSE9CTWJHLWQyNkdDeHJSY09PaVZUcGItTlFEdHBHaGVlQ3k0ekRQMjdvUm1EMkhlMG1pY29kQmtSVUNrR2dRS0VFRmdNOHpCaGRkeVU3WFB3NlE?oc=5,,,"PrashantNews
Two women died after falling into a deep gorge while trying to escape a bear attack in Chamoli district of Uttarakhand, officials said.
The incident occurred on Friday morning near Sidelii village in Pokhari tehsil when the two women, who were sisters in law in relations, had gone into the Boga forest to collect fodder for their cattle.
According to officials, a bear suddenly attacked the women, identified as Vimla Devi, 55, and her sister-in-law Lakshmi Devi, 42. In an attempt to escape the animal, both women fell several hundred feet down a steep rocky slope.
Villagers rushed the injured women to the Community Health Centre in Pokhari, where Vimla Devi was found to be in critical condition. Despite treatment, she succumbed to her injuries.
Lakshmi Devi, who had also sustained serious injuries was given initial treatment at the health centre and referred to the Base Hospital in Srinagar Garhwal.
Pokhari Tehsildar Ramkishor Dhyani said that a helicopter had been arranged on the directions of the Chamoli District Magistrate to airlift the critically injured woman. However, the helicopter could not take off because of adverse weather conditions.
Lakshmi Devi was subsequently taken to the Base Hospital by ambulance, but she too succumbed to her injuries during treatment.
Vimla Devi’s husband Chandrashekhar told reporters in Pokhari that his wife and Lakshmi Devi had gone to the Boga forest to cut grass when the bear suddenly attacked them.
The incident has once again highlighted the risks faced by villagers in Uttarakhand where people frequently enter forests to collect fodder and firewood and face encounters with wild animals.","success(trafilatura, resolved_via_decode, 1665 chars)"
"Wild Bear creates panic, chases locals and cows in Rishikesh; video goes viral - India TV News",2026-01-02,Uttarakhand,,Asiatic Black Bear,Human Injury,0.0,0.0,0.0,"A wild bear chased locals and cows in Rishikesh, causing panic.",0.98,single_incident,,India TV News,https://news.google.?oc=5,,,"Hill regions are known for their beauty but also for their harsh realities. Residents often face natural hazards and the constant threat of wild animals. In such an incident, chaos erupted in the pilgrimage town of Rishikesh when a wild bear entered a residential locality as preparations were underway to welcome the New Year. The incident took place on Wednesday (December 31) night in the Shyampur Hat Road area, and the CCTV footage surfaced on Friday. The sudden appearance of the bear just two hours before the New Year celebrations created fear across the town. The entire sequence was captured on CCTV and has now gone viral on social media.
What the viral video reveals
The incident occurred near Shyampur Hat Road around 10 pm on December 31. The video shows two men casually strolling on the street, unaware of what lies ahead. A few cows are also seen wandering peacefully behind them. As the duo reaches the corner of the lane, a bear suddenly appears in front of them. Stunned and terrified, the two immediately turned back and ran for safety. The cows also scattered in fear. The bear was seen running behind them, adding to the panic captured in the clip.
Forest department unable to trace the bear
After the video went viral on social media, locals immediately alerted the Forest Department. However, officials have not yet been able to trace the bear or identify the route it used to reach the urban settlement. CCTV footage confirmed the animal's presence in the area, prompting authorities to strengthen patrolling and advisory measures for residents.
What to do if you spot a bear
It is advised to follow essential safety guidelines to avoid danger during a bear encounter:
- Stay calm: Loud noise or running suddenly can provoke the bear.
- Slowly move back: Do not turn your back, maintain eye contact and step away gradually.
- Make yourself appear bigger: Raise your arms or open a jacket so the bear does not see you as weak.
- Protect yourself if attacked: Lie down on the ground, cover your stomach and protect your head with your arms.
- Call for help: Contact emergency services at 112 or inform the local wildlife department.
